(vector (map (:db/id -1), (:adr/id "ADR-2608137400"), (:adr/title "Six baseline entries that were never gone, and a rename ledger adjudicated by id"), (:adr/status "accepted"), (:adr/date "2026-08-13"), (:adr/supersedes ""), (:adr/superseded-by ""), (:adr/body "# ADR-2608137400: six baseline entries that were never gone, and a rename ledger adjudicated by id\n\nTwo small red checks, both red for a reason about the check rather than about the\nworkspace. Same family as ADR-2608136000, approached from the other side: that ADR\ncollected checks which could not measure and returned the PASS value. These two\nmeasured something, returned a FAIL value, and named the wrong thing.\n\n## 1. `docs-edn-only.cljs verify` reported six .md as gone. None was gone.\n\nThe run ended:\n\n    === BASELINE IS STALE - these .md files are gone, remove them from known-md-files ===\n      90-docs/kura/README.md\n      90-docs/religious-community/README.md\n      90-docs/religious-community/queries/guide.md\n      90-docs/security-gates/evidence/EVIDENCE-SUMMARY.md\n      90-docs/security-gates/evidence/INDEX.md\n      90-docs/security/kagi-external-crypto-review-package-20260718.md\n    verify: FAIL\n\nThe instruction in that message is to prune them. Acting on it would have deleted\nsix true entries from the ratchet, which is the single thing the baseline exists\nto prevent.\n\n**Disposition of each of the six: none of deleted, converted or moved. All six are\npresent, unmodified, in `origin/main`.** Verified by listing the paths out of the\nremote-tracking tree rather than the working copy:\n\n    git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main -- 90-docs/kura 90-docs/religious-community \\\n      90-docs/security-gates/evidence 90-docs/security | grep '.md$'\n    -> all six printed\n\nThey were absent only from the working tree. This checkout is cone-mode sparse and\nthe four directories holding them sit outside the cone; `git ls-files -v` shows all\nsix carrying the `S` (skip-worktree) bit. `verify!` enumerated with `file-seq` over\nthe working tree, so a file that git is deliberately not writing to disk was\nindistinguishable from a file that had been removed from the repository.\n\nMeasured in this worktree: 70 of 76 .md and 2,340 of 2,505 .edn were visible. The\nheader line reported `md=70 edn=2340 parse-errors=0`, in which the `0` describes\n93% of the corpus and reads exactly like a `0` over all of it.\n\n### This is the second occurrence, from one cause, in both directions\n\nThe same six files were reported as NEW for a week (2026-07-31 -> 2026-08-08),\nbecause the baseline itself had been captured from a sparse worktree; they were\nadded in e5606cf2d47. `git log -S` confirms it is the identical set of six.\nSo the same defect produced `these are new` and then `these are gone` about the\nsame six files, and both times the honest answer was `these are fine`.\n\nThe remedy taken after the first occurrence was a prose warning in the\n`known-md-files` docstring to regenerate only from a full checkout. It did not\nhold, and could not have: the run that is about to get the wrong answer does not\nread the docstring. A partial view does not report that it is partial.\n\n### Fix\n\nEnumerate from git, not from the filesystem:\n\n    git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard -- 90-docs\n\n`--cached` reads the index, so the answer no longer depends on which paths the cone\nincludes; `--others --exclude-standard` still catches a newly written .md that has\nnot been committed, which is the case the check is for.\n\nThat fixes the .md baseline completely, because it is a question about which files\nthe repository contains. It does NOT fix the .edn checks, which read file CONTENT:\ncontent that is not in this working tree cannot be parsed. So those are split out\nand reported, and the run refuses to claim a pass:\n\n  - header now reads `edn=<scanned>/<listed>`, so a subset cannot be misread as the whole\n  - the unreadable files are listed, with the one-line remedy\n  - **exit 2** - neither 0 nor 1 - when the corpus was incomplete. A real defect still\n    outranks it and exits 1, because every check that did run saw what it reported.\n\n### Break and restore (verbatim)\n\nBaseline green, full 90-docs cone, 2,505 of 2,505 read:\n\n    verify: md=76 edn=2505/2505 parse-errors=0 not-tx=7 split-strings=0 path-md-refs=20 source-format-residue=0\n    verify: OK - no NEW breakage (0 known-unparseable and 76 un-migrated .md accepted as baselines; nothing else is wrong)\n    EXIT=0\n\nDeliberate break - one .md added under 90-docs, absent from the baseline, left\nuntracked so the `--others` half of the enumeration is what catches it:\n\n    $ cat 90-docs/zzz-deliberate-break-probe.md   # 3 lines, temporary\n    verify: md=77 edn=2505/2505 parse-errors=0 not-tx=7 split-strings=0 path-md-refs=20 source-format-residue=0\n    === NEW .md UNDER 90-docs (not in the accepted baseline) ===\n      EDN is the source of truth here; a new .md moves the migration backwards.\n      90-docs/zzz-deliberate-break-probe.md\n    verify: FAIL\n    EXIT=1\n\nThe break names the file that was broken and nothing else: `md` moved 76 -> 77 and\nevery other count on the header line is byte-identical to the green run\n(edn 2505/2505, parse-errors 0, not-tx 7, split-strings 0, path-md-refs 20,\nsource-format-residue 0). ADR-2608136000 records four occasions that day of a\nbreak appearing to succeed while actually exercising a different path; this one was\nchecked against that.\n\nRestore - probe removed:\n\n    verify: md=76 edn=2505/2505 parse-errors=0 not-tx=7 split-strings=0 path-md-refs=20 source-format-residue=0\n    verify: OK - no NEW breakage (...)\n    EXIT=0\n\n    $ diff <green-before> <green-after>  ->  IDENTICAL\n\nAnd the refusal path, from the sparse cone this session started in:\n\n    verify: md=76 edn=2340/2505 parse-errors=0 ...\n    === 165 .edn ARE IN GIT BUT NOT IN THIS WORKING TREE ===\n      Fix: git sparse-checkout add 90-docs   (or run from a full checkout)\n    verify: CANNOT ANSWER - 165 of 2505 .edn under 90-docs were not readable here.\n    EXIT=2\n\nNote `md=76` there: the .md baseline is answered correctly even from the sparse\ncone, which is the point of moving it to the index. The six false alarms are gone.\n\nWhen the 165 previously-unreadable files were finally read, they parsed clean -\nparse-errors 0, split-strings 0. Nothing was hiding in them. That is the outcome to\nexpect most of the time, and it is not a reason to keep not looking.\n\n## 2. `manifest/repos.edn` - the ledger was not missing the transfer\n\nThe reported finding was that repos.edn recorded the rename of\n`ai-gftd-ghosthacker-shiropico` but not the later org transfer to com-junkawasaki.\nMeasured, it records both, as two separate entries:\n\n    orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-ghosthacker-shiropico -> orgs/gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico\n    orgs/gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico         -> orgs/com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico\n\nBoth statements are true. What does not happen is their composition:\n`canonical-path` in `scripts/gen-west-manifest.cljs` is a single `get`, one hop, so\nresolving the oldest name stops on the middle - a path that is nobody's current\nlocation. Eight such chains exist among the 463 overrides.\n\n### Why this one and not the other seven\n\nOnly one of the eight chain heads exists on disk: `orgs/gftdcojp/`\n`ai-gftd-ghosthacker-shiropico`, left behind as an untracked directory. The\ngenerator canonicalises paths it finds by scanning, so the other seven are\nunreachable and latent. That is why the survey caught exactly this one.\n\n### The obvious fix would have been wrong\n\nThe tempting repair is to make `canonical-path` transitive, fixing all eight in one\nplace. Checked against GitHub repository ids, seven of the eight chains have head\nand terminal resolving to the same id. The eighth does not:\n\n    orgs/kotoba-lang/ui              -> id 1286691203  kotoba-lang/kami-engine-hud\n    orgs/kotoba-lang/kami-engine-app-sdk -> id 1287091135  kotoba-lang/kami-engine-app-sdk\n\nTwo different repositories. A transitive resolver would have collapsed `ui` onto a\nrepo it is not, which is precisely the hazard the `reclaimed-paths` set in that\nfile already warns about: an old path can be deliberately reclaimed later, and the\nhistorical rename must not fold the new project back into its former target. The\nsingle-hop resolver was protecting that case. Left alone.\n\n### Correction applied\n\nOne entry, repointed onto the repository it names, verified by id first - the old\nname, the recorded middle and the new location all resolve to the same repository:\n\n    gh api repos/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-ghosthacker-shiropico -> 1285918569 com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico\n    gh api repos/gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico         -> 1285918569 com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico\n    gh api repos/com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico  -> 1285918569 com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico\n\n    - orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-ghosthacker-shiropico -> orgs/gftdcojp/ghosthacker-shiropico\n    + orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-ghosthacker-shiropico -> orgs/com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico\n\nOne word in the file; entry count unchanged at 463; chains 8 -> 7. The second entry\nis kept - it is a true record of the transfer and is what resolves the middle name.\nwest.yml already emits the correct path (`orgs/com-junkawasaki/ghosthacker-shiropico`,\nremote com-junkawasaki) and is unaffected.\n\n## Verdict: the ledger can be machine-checked against ids, and must not be scheduled\n\n**A rename ledger is not an identity.** Identity is the GitHub repository id; the\nnames are aliases the redirect keeps answering, which is exactly why a stale ledger\nentry stays plausible - every name in the chain above still resolves. Reading the\nledger cannot detect that one of them is no longer canonical. Only the id can.\n\nThe check is worth having, and this session is the evidence: it found the real\ndefect AND stopped the plausible fix from corrupting `kotoba-lang/ui`. Without\nids, a `flatten all the chains` cleanup looks obviously correct and is not.\n\n**It should not go on a schedule.** `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` excludes the\n`gh` class in terms, at line 89: anything needing gh is rate-limited and shares one\ntoken with every concurrent agent session on this machine. That exclusion is drawn\non the shared-token property, not on cost, so making a check cheaper is not grounds\nto overturn it - a cheap check on a schedule still spends the shared token on a\ntimer nobody is watching. `verify-duplicate-registrations` is already classed there\nfor doing exactly this (`gh api repos/<slug>` per candidate to confirm two paths are\none upstream by id). This belongs in the same place: the credentialed operator path,\nrun on demand - at registration of a rename or transfer, and during audits.\n\nThat is not a counsel of doing nothing on a schedule, because the work splits:\n\n  - **Finding candidates is free and needs no token.** `a value in :path-overrides\n    that is itself a key` is pure EDN over one file. It found all eight chains in\n    milliseconds, with no network. It is safe to schedule and it is the input to\n    the expensive step.\n  - **Adjudicating a candidate needs the id, and therefore gh.** Only this step\n    distinguishes the seven stale hops from the one genuine reclaim, and only this\n    step needs the shared token.\n\nSplitting them keeps the scheduled half free and leaves exactly the irreducible\npart on the operator path, where a human is present for the answer. Registering the\nfree half as a detector is deliberately NOT done here: adding to that registry is a\ndecision about the schedule and ADR-2608132600 shows what it costs when a `:why`\nis written from the finding text rather than from measurement. It should be\nproposed with its own timing measured.\n\nThe same id-based method already has precedent in this workspace for this exact\nclass: ADR-2608132600 resolved three org transfers that had drifted out from under\na path-keyed exemption list by id (1275051908 / 1281275923 / 1136866292).\n\n## Consequences\n\n  - `docs-edn-only.cljs verify` answers the .md baseline correctly from any\n    checkout, sparse or full, and stops manufacturing false `gone` reports.\n  - It gains a third outcome. Callers that treat non-zero as failure are unaffected;\n    a reader can now tell `nothing is wrong` from `I could not look`.\n  - No consumer had to change: the verifier is referenced only in comments and in\n    the message text of `.claude/hooks/docs-edn-parse-guard.cljs`. No fleet gate\n    runs it, so the new exit code breaks nothing.\n  - Six true baseline entries survive that a literal reading of the old output\n    would have deleted.\n  - Seven latent path-override chains remain, six of them same-id and unreachable\n    by scan, one (`kotoba-lang/ui`) that must not be flattened. Recorded here rather\n    than changed, because none is currently reachable and each needs its own id\n    check before being touched.\n  - Measured under load average 62-90 from concurrent work; verify takes ~50s on\n    the sparse tree and the 16 `gh` id lookups took 32s warm.")))